WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.680
- What is going on?

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.140
The second and third largest bank failures

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.780
in U.S. history happened
in a three-day period,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.970
and it's got investors, regulators,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.430
and bank customers
understandably concerned.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.590
There is still a lot of
information we don't have

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.320
about the collapse of Signature
Bank, Silicon Valley Bank,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.270
and others, but what we do know

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.070
is there's plenty of reason to have faith

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.500
that the U.S. banking system will be okay.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.050
Unfortunately, there's also
plenty of reason to worry

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.060
that some of the issues
that brought down Signature

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.080
and SVB could be evidence of
a bigger systemic problem.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.220
I'll explain.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.310
First, let's talk about
the big picture risks.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.170
Silicon Valley Bank was invested in many

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.090
of the same assets as other large banks,

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.490
U.S. treasuries,
mortgage-backed securities,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.710
and other bonds that
have seen big declines

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.150
in value over the past year.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.620
Those are actually the safe assets

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.200
that the Federal Reserve
requires banks to hold.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.580
Many banks are invested in things

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.370
with much higher risk
profiles like stocks,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.170
leveraged loans and derivatives.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.120
Those have also seen big losses,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.820
and banks often use borrowed
money to make those investments

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.620
in an effort to increase their returns.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.230
The Fed lowered the reserve
requirement ratio for banks

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.590
to zero in March 2020 and
has still not raised it,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.020
meaning banks that hold
billions in deposits

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.990
can hold as little as $40 million in cash.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.060
U.S. banks had unrealized losses

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.660
of more than $600 billion
at the end of 2022,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.630
and things haven't gotten much
better in markets since then.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.640
If depositors start to
demand their money back,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.590
lots of these Banks
could need to sell assets

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.750
at a loss to meet those demands

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.340
or they might try to raise money

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.280
through stock or bond sales like SVB did,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.700
and that could lead to
more runs on more banks,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.950
but there is some good news.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.630
Banks have stronger regulations

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.790
than they did before the
global financial crisis.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.010
The nation's largest
banks are regularly put

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.200
through so-called stress tests by the Fed

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.510
to determine whether they
could withstand a crisis,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.090
and the U.S. banks determine
to be systemically important

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.620
have all passed.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.370
There's also a lot more money
deposited in fewer banks now.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.200
Between 2010 and 2020,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.350
the number of banking
institutions dropped by almost 30%

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.050
and the five biggest
banks now hold nearly half

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.330
of all FDIC-insured deposits.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.810
That's not necessarily
great for consumer choice,

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.660
but it does mean that the
bigger banks are better able

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.240
to fortify themselves
against short-term panic

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.970
and market losses to stay in business.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:18.870
Another reason to think

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.790
that this is not a wide-ranging problem

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.680
is that Silicon Valley Bank had what folks

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.500
on Wall Street called
idiosyncratic problems,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.900
meaning issues that were unique to them.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.450
As its name suggests, Silicon Valley Bank

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.420
did a lot of business with
venture capital firms, startups,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.030
and others in Silicon Valley,

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.800
home of the U.S. tech industry.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.930
Tech investment has
gotten battered this year

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.110
as investors have been spooked
by tumbling stock prices.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.840
The Fed's rate hiking cycle
has been especially damaging

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.540
for shares of tech companies,
because many are relying

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.210
on making profits far off in the future.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.800
Higher interest rates today

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.810
eat into those potential future profits

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.940
and make tech companies
look less valuable.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.610
Plus, it costs more for
venture capital firms

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.500
and other investors to borrow money

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.970
to put into these companies

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.580
that are not turning a
profit in many cases,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.650
so many of the firms and individuals

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.890
who had money held in
SVB were taking it out

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.600
rather than putting more in.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.390
SVB also had invested
big in mortgage debt,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.280
much of it coming at
the top of the market,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.310
and home prices have generally
lost value since then,

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.590
and the bank purchased
limited market hedges

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.010
like swaps that could
have reduced their losses

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.050
in the event of big downturns
like the ones we've seen.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.910
So are we in for more trouble?

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.280
There's no way to know for
sure, but if you've got less

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.150
than $250,000 in an
FDIC-insured bank account,

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.920
there's no reason to take your money out.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.740
As we've seen recently, that's
insured by the government,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.970
and even if banks fail,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.540
depositors below the
limit will be made whole.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.790
(pleasant synth music)

